    <p class="irmaa-problem">
      Receiving a letter demanding an extra $81 to $444 per month on top of your already expensive Medicare Part B premium is a shock — especially after you've just retired on a fixed income. What most seniors don't know is that Social Security uses tax return data from two years ago to set these surcharges, meaning retirees often pay IRMAA rates based on their high working income for the first one to two years of retirement. This free wizard checks your specific situation against the eight official qualifying Life-Changing Events in minutes.
